---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Rule from '../components/Rule.astro';
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';
import PageNext from '../components/PageNext.astro';
---

<Layout title="Hard rules" pathname="/hard-rules">
  <section class="page-head">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="eyebrow-chain">
        <span class="eyebrow">Hard rules</span>
        <span class="eyebrow-sep">·</span>
        <span class="eyebrow-dim">the identity</span>
        <span class="eyebrow-sep">·</span>
        <span class="eyebrow-dim">8 + 1 invariants</span>
      </div>
      <h1>The 8+1 hard rules.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        These aren't policy on top of features. They're the things every feature has to be compatible with to exist at all. <span class="verb">maddu doctor</span> proves them on every install and every upgrade. If a repo violates one, it isn't a Máddu repo.
      </p>

      <dl class="hr-stats">
        <div><dt>Hard rules</dt><dd>8</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Permanent additions</dt><dd>1</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Doctor gates total</dt><dd>60</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Auto-repair</dt><dd>0</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <header class="section-head">
        <span class="eyebrow">§ 01 · The rules</span>
        <h2>Each one is named, enforced, and load-bearing.</h2>
      </header>

      <div class="rule-stack">
        <Rule num="1" title="Files-only state" enforcedBy="doctor · files-only-state · layout-refusal">
          <p>Every state write is append-only NDJSON, a rebuildable JSON projection, or a typed Markdown/JSON definition. <strong>No SQLite, no embedded DB, no hosted DB.</strong> If you can't <code>cat</code> it, Máddu didn't write it. Portability and audit are downstream of this single decision.</p>
        </Rule>
        <Rule num="2" title="Append-only event spine" enforcedBy="maddu spine verify · plan-state-derivable · kanban-coherent">
          <p>
            <code>.maddu/events/*.ndjson</code> is the single source of truth. Projections rebuild from it and are <em>never</em> authoritative. Derived ≠ projected — auto-decisions append their own event with <code>triggered_by</code>. <strong>Verifiable, not declared.</strong> <code>maddu spine verify</code> checks; <strong>there is no auto-repair, ever.</strong>
          </p>
        </Rule>
        <Rule num="3" title="No hosted backends" enforcedBy="architectural · no-network-app-code">
          <p>Provider APIs are called from local subprocess workers only. <strong>No SaaS, no telemetry, no relay, no "Máddu Cloud."</strong> A bridge listens on localhost and dies when you kill the process. The cockpit is a static page served by that bridge. Nothing leaves the machine.</p>
        </Rule>
        <Rule num="4" title="No broad new dependencies" enforcedBy="dependency-freshness · dep-pinning-respected">
          <p>Node stdlib where possible. The <code>dependencies</code> block stays empty or narrowly-scoped. Every dep is a supply-chain surface; every supply-chain surface needs <code>maddu trust</code> provenance. The cheapest defense is the dep you didn't take.</p>
        </Rule>
        <Rule num="5" title="No provider SDKs in app code" enforcedBy="layout-refusal · secret-scan-active">
          <p>Bridge, cockpit, harness — none of them imports <code>anthropic</code>, <code>openai</code>, <code>@google/generative-ai</code>, or any other provider SDK. Provider calls live <em>only</em> in worker subprocesses, with credentials injected at spawn. Layout-refusal kills doctor if an SDK leaks into app code.</p>
        </Rule>
        <Rule num="6" title="No token export" enforcedBy="device-bound paths · export/import contracts">
          <p>
            OAuth tokens live in OS device-bound paths: <code>~/.config/maddu/auth/</code> (Linux/macOS) or <code>%APPDATA%\maddu\auth\</code> (Windows). <code>maddu export</code> scrubs them. <code>maddu import</code> refuses to overwrite them. <strong>No cross-machine token sync, by design.</strong>
          </p>
        </Rule>
        <Rule num="7" title="Three-layer brand boundary" enforcedBy="architectural">
          <p>Framework shell brand (Máddu) / app brand (the consumer project) / content brand (whatever the project ships) never mix. The cockpit's <code>tokens.css</code> is owned by Máddu and must not be referenced from app or content code. This keeps the orchestration layer recognizable across every project that adopts it.</p>
        </Rule>
        <Rule num="8" title="Lane ownership" enforcedBy="rule-8-team-lane-disjoint · lane-force-discipline">
          <p>Claim a lane before editing its file area. <strong>No two agents may hold the same lane concurrently.</strong> Cross-lane work goes through the mailbox bus (<code>.maddu/lanes/&lt;lane&gt;/mailbox.ndjson</code>), <em>not</em> shared mutation. Force-claims leave a contested-claim audit trail. This is concurrency without locks.</p>
        </Rule>
        <Rule num="9" title="Every auto-trigger crosses the gauntlet" enforcedBy="strict-mode-approval-active · triggers.json">
          <p>
            Permanent since v0.19.0. No spine-, state-, or workspace-mutating command auto-fires without: a <code>tier:'mutating'</code> entry, an allowlist entry in <code>.maddu/config/triggers.json</code>, a respected cooldown, and a <code>TRIGGER_FIRED</code> event carrying <code>triggered_by</code> provenance.
          </p>
          <p>This is the rule that prevents the "confidently wrong, unsupervised, running away" failure mode. <a href="/governance">Open the gauntlet →</a></p>
        </Rule>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <header class="section-head">
        <span class="eyebrow eyebrow-danger">§ 02 · What's deliberately absent</span>
        <h2>The "no"s that make the "yes"es load-bearing.</h2>
        <p>None of these are missing features. Each is a deliberate decision about what Máddu refuses to become.</p>
      </header>

      <ul class="absent-stack">
        <li>
          <span class="absent-no">no</span>
          <div><strong>Scheduler thread.</strong> <span class="dim">The spine is the WAL (write-ahead log).</span></div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="absent-no">no</span>
          <div><strong>Worker queue.</strong> <span class="dim">Lane claims are the lock.</span></div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="absent-no">no</span>
          <div><strong>Mutex layer.</strong> <span class="dim">Lane discipline replaces it at a higher altitude.</span></div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="absent-no">no</span>
          <div><strong>Websocket.</strong> <span class="dim">Long-poll is enough; a websocket would add a stateful connection surface for nothing.</span></div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="absent-no">no</span>
          <div><strong>SaaS, telemetry, or relay.</strong> <span class="dim">Nothing leaves the machine.</span></div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="absent-no">no</span>
          <div><strong>Auto-repair, ever.</strong> <span class="dim">A system that silently heals can silently hide an attack.</span></div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="absent-no">no</span>
          <div><strong>Cross-machine token sync.</strong> <span class="dim">Device-bound or nothing.</span></div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="absent-no">no</span>
          <div><strong>Hermes pattern.</strong> <span class="dim">A hosted webhook holding many users' bot tokens is the failure mode the rest of the rules exist to prevent.</span></div>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <Callout tone="ok" title="Read this as a posture, not a wishlist" tag="charter">
        <p>The hard rules describe what Máddu <em>is</em>. The absences describe what it <em>refuses to be</em>. Together they're the identity. Charter source of truth lives in the framework repo at <code>docs/charter.md</code>.</p>
      </Callout>
    </div>
  </section>

  <PageNext
    eyebrow="§ 03 · Continue"
    title="Capabilities — every verb earns its place."
    hint="The verb ontology. 62 verbs across 8 purpose areas. One charter that flags drift."
    href="/capabilities"
    tone="accent"
  />
</Layout>

<style>
  .page-head { padding: var(--m-space-16) 0 var(--m-space-8); }
  .lede { font-size: 17px; color: var(--m-fg-0); max-width: 64ch; }
  .eyebrow-danger { color: var(--m-danger); }

  .hr-stats {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(140px, 1fr));
    gap: var(--m-space-5);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--m-line-soft);
    padding-top: var(--m-space-5);
    margin: var(--m-space-6) 0 0;
    max-width: 620px;
  }
  .hr-stats div { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 4px; }
  .hr-stats dt {
    font-family: var(--m-font-mono);
    font-size: 10px;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--m-fg-3);
  }
  .hr-stats dd {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--m-font-cond);
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: 28px;
    color: var(--m-accent);
    line-height: 1;
  }

  .rule-stack {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    gap: var(--m-space-3);
  }
  @media (min-width: 880px) {
    .rule-stack { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; }
  }

  .absent-stack {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    gap: var(--m-space-2);
  }
  .absent-stack li {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 60px 1fr;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: var(--m-space-3);
    padding: var(--m-space-3) var(--m-space-4);
    background: var(--m-bg-2);
    border: 1px solid var(--m-line);
    border-left: 2px solid var(--m-danger);
    border-radius: var(--m-radius);
  }
  .absent-stack .absent-no {
    color: var(--m-danger);
    font-weight: 600;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    font-family: var(--m-font-mono);
    font-size: 11px;
  }
  .absent-stack strong { color: var(--m-fg-0); }
  .absent-stack .dim { color: var(--m-fg-3); margin-left: 8px; }
</style>
